p.src:
If the initial state is an energy eigenstate, its evolution is $\ket{E;t}
=
\exp\left(-\frac{iEt}{\hbar}\right)\ket{E}.$ It remains the same energy eigenvector and acquires only a phase. (The minus sign is fixed by the preceding derivation of $U(t)=\exp(-iHt/\hbar)$. A later spoken rendering of the phase has the opposite sign and is inconsistent with that derivation.)

